    statement: "Weight exfiltration containment defeats a copy of a frontier model leaving its environment, whether by an insider, an intruder or the model itself, by making the copy physically slow, visible and interruptible at the network boundary.",
    origin: "RAND's Securing AI Model Weights (2024) set out the threat and the security levels, and Anthropic described egress bandwidth controls, two party controls and endpoint controls as part of its ASL-3 security standard in May 2025. The specific case of a model exfiltrating itself is discussed in the control and scaling policy literature. Few organisations have described their implementation, and none has published a detection event, which makes the model frontier.",
    structure: "The weight boundary (every system that can hold plaintext weights) is enclosed in a network perimeter whose total egress bandwidth is capped at a rate that makes copying the full weights take a long time (days or more), measured against the model's size. Egress is permitted only to named destinations through proxies that log volume per destination and per workload identity. Volume monitoring compares cumulative egress against the weight size and alerts at a small fraction; a hard cutoff isolates the perimeter when a threshold is reached. Inside the perimeter, weights are chunked and encrypted so that a partial copy is useless, and canary weights or watermarks allow a leaked copy to be identified. Insider paths (removable media, console access, printing) are closed or two person controlled. For agentic models with tool access, the model's own tools are inside the perimeter and subject to the same caps, so self exfiltration is bounded by the same bandwidth.",
